    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mapped normal fault traces from two regions in North America (south-central Oregon and Canyonlands National Park, Utah) show the same relationship between step-sense, oblique-slip sense, and the geometry of fault linkage: right-stepping, right-oblique fault pairs and left-stepping, left-oblique fault pairs breach at the bottom of the relay ramp; left-stepping, right-oblique fault pairs breach at the top of the relay ramp. Results from numerical modeling corroborate these observations: When the sense of step and the sense of oblique slip are the same, deformation may increase in the region of the front-segment tip, promoting lower-ramp deformation. For the opposite case, when the sense of step and the sense of oblique slip are opposite, models show that deformation may increase in the region of the rear-segment tip, promoting upper-ramp deformation. Thus, the sense of step between echelon normal faults relative to the sense of oblique slip influences the geometry of subsequent fault linkage.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
